#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Citations the aya settles where the damage is not a dot.

The September 2026 collation read every bracketed span in both text fields of
all 56 lessons against the Warsh reference and graded 6,320 divergences A.
2,267 of them are a dot and were written by
scripts/repair-quranic-collation-dotting.py.  The remaining 4,053 need a letter
from outside the confusion classes, a letter put back, a letter taken out, or a
word boundary moved.  The dot classes were a property of the earlier scripts,
not of the evidence, so this pass takes the 3,969 that are letters rather than
boundaries and stops where its own tests stop.

WHAT REPLACES THE DOT TEST

The dotting pass could lean on a hard criterion: the printed word had to reach
the aya word by dots alone, which is a test no alignment error survives.
Outside the classes there is no such test, so the gate is a bounded edit
distance measured after folding the confusion classes together and dropping
alifs -- a swap inside a class costs nothing, every other operation costs one.
A word of three folded letters or fewer may stand one operation from its
target; a longer word may stand two.  Past that the printed word stops being
damage of the aya word and starts being a different word, and nothing in the
span can tell those apart.  233 sites are refused on that line alone.

The target itself is never read off the skeleton.  It is the aya word FACING
the printed word when the whole span is aligned against the aya, and a site
whose span does not close around the word is refused: 1,441 of them, the
largest single refusal in this pass and mostly spans the scan damaged past
alignment.

EVERY GUARD REPORTS ITS DENOMINATOR

The first dotting pass reported that its safeguard had passed, on 1,789 sites
where it had found nothing to compare.  Nothing in its output told that from
agreement, and thirteen wrong words reached primary text.  So each guard here
prints how many sites it EVALUATED beside how many it passed, and a site with
no evidence is counted unevaluated, never clear.

  A.  Does this pass name the word the collation names?
      evaluated 2,232 of 2,232 -- passed 2,158 -- refused 74 -- unevaluated 0.

  B.  Does a better-corroborated aya name a different word?  Every candidate
      aya is scored by how many of the span\'s OTHER words stand in it.
      evaluated 2,156 of the 2,158 reaching it -- passed 2,156 -- refused 0 --
      unevaluated 2, both because no rival aya could hold the word at all.

  C.  Where more than one word answers, does the span pick between them?
      evaluated 2,157 of 2,158 -- passed 1,985 -- refused 172 -- unevaluated 1.
      Of the 2,157: 1,812 unanimous, one word answering; 220 a real tie, of
      which the span picked 173.  125 of the 172 refusals are sites where more
      than 60 ayat corroborate the span equally well, which is not a tie to
      break but a span that says nothing.

      Guard C cannot be run here the way it was run on the dots.  There the
      damage left the skeleton intact, so every aya holding that skeleton was a
      candidate and there were rarely more than a few dozen.  Here a word two
      operations from `min` is two operations from half the mushaf, and the
      candidate lists run past 5,000.  So the guard scores every candidate by
      how many of the span\'s own words it holds and aligns only those within
      one of the best.  An aya sharing nothing with the span is not a rival.
      That is a weaker guard than the dotting pass had, and it is said here
      rather than buried.

THE NEIGHBOUR RULE

A paragraph in this commentary walks consecutive ayat, bracketed lemma by
lemma.  So where a span is ambiguous, the bracketed spans on either side of it
in the same paragraph say which sura is being read and where in it.  Two
conditions, both required: the neighbours\' own attributions must be ones
scripts/match-verses.js SHIPS -- `substring`, `pair` and `enclosed`, never
`fuzzy` or `ambiguous`, which are its guesses -- and a shipped attribution must
stand on BOTH sides in the same paragraph.  The run they bracket is then the
only place a candidate may come from, and the candidates left have to answer
with one word or the site stays refused.

Measured on the dotting pass\'s 137 refusals before anything was written: 128
are exact ties, 37 of them in footnotes the matcher never reads.  The rule
settles 15, of which 5 are the `fa-lam`/`qul` shape that accounts for 38 of the
ties.  76 it cannot reach, every one of them for want of a shipped attribution
on both sides.  Where it did reach, it never once found the neighbours naming
two suras and never once left two words standing.  Relaxing it to any two
shipped attributions in the paragraph would settle 24 and refuse 9 more on
evidence; relaxing it to one would settle 44 and refuse 19.  Neither is taken:
a paragraph quotes cross-references as well as the aya it is reading, so a run
that does not bracket the span is not evidence about it.

14 of those 15 are written here -- the fifteenth, Lesson 33 `yahbi` for
`yuhyi` at Q 20:74, is in the tables too -- and they are marked TIE.  On this
pass\'s own 172 refusals the rule reached 24 and settled 19.

ONE WORD ALREADY IN THE TREE IS CORRECTED

Lesson 19 arabicBody, the span `idh ya`dun FI l-sabt` of Q 7:163.  The dotting
pass wrote `min` there on the collation\'s attribution to Q 2:133.  The
paragraph is Q 7:160-163 throughout and the very next bracketed span is Q 7:163
`wa-yawma la yasbitun`, so the word is `fi`: mim for fa, which is not a dot and
is why the earlier `mi`/`fi` pass left it.  The row carries the committed form
`min` beside the scan\'s `mi` so that a tree holding either reaches `fi`, and
the dotting table\'s row for that site is deleted.

The same neighbour test was then run over all 2,103 sites the dotting pass
holds.  264 of them sit in a paragraph that brackets a run at all; the run
holds the cited aya at 215 and denies it at 49.  Of those 49, 33 have an
in-run aya carrying the very same word, 16 are the commentary quoting a
cross-reference, and NONE has an in-run aya carrying a different word.  The
one wrong word above was found by hand, not by that test, which is worth
knowing about the test.

WHAT IS NOT WRITTEN, AND WHY

  110  the word needs a letter moved AND another letter changed.  Two edits in
       one word is where a mark ends up on a letter that never carried it.
   79  a hamza stands in the printed word or the aya word.  A standalone hamza
       is not a letter in the skeleton, so a seat the scan misread as a
       consonant reads here as a letter too many: `tus\'alun` scanned as
       `tustalun` looks like a ta to delete and is not.
   57  a letter has to come OUT.  Every one was built and read, and the class
       is unsafe for exactly the reason above.
   32  the restored consonant closes the word and the mushaf vowels the letter
       before the gap differently from the printing, so the mark in the scan
       may be the case ending and belong to the restored letter.  `al-lLi` for
       `allahi` is the shape.
   21  an alif stands inside the letters being rewritten.  Alif practice is the
       printing\'s and is not a defect; an alif caught up in someone else\'s
       repair cannot be decided here.
   16  the word is further from its target than one letter in and out.
   58  the printed word and the aya word differ ONLY in alif practice.
   84  word boundaries -- 63 joins and 21 splits, untouched.
2,655  grade B, where paraphrase and damage cannot be told apart.  AK.

ON THE ANCHORS

Neighbours are compared through A.dotfold, which merges eleven confusable
classes and collapses 28 letters to 14.  Outside the dot classes a repaired
neighbour does NOT fold back to what an anchor stored, so sites falling on
adjacent words are minted as one row covering the run: 132 of these rows cover
two words apiece.  1,480 of 1,574 rows resolve at width 5 and none is
ambiguous at any width.

Usage: dry run by default; pass --write to apply. Idempotent.
Sites are named by content, not by offset: see scripts/repair_anchors.py.
"""
